{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'openspec instructions proposal --change resilient-teardown-cleanup --json && grep -RInE \"teardown_request|teardown_appcontext|appcontext_tearing_down|request_tearing_down\" src/flask tests | head -n 220'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"changeName\": \"resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"artifactId\": \"proposal\",\n  \"schemaName\": \"spec-driven\",\n  \"changeDir\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup\",\n  \"planningHome\": {\n    \"kind\": \"repo\",\n    \"root\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"changesDir\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes\",\n    \"defaultSchema\": \"spec-driven\"\n  },\n  \"outputPath\": \"proposal.md\",\n  \"resolvedOutputPath\": \"/workspace/openspec/changes/resilient-teardown-cleanup/proposal.md\",\n  \"existingOutputPaths\": [],\n  \"description\": \"Initial proposal document outlining the change\",\n  \"instruction\": \"Create the proposal document that establishes WHY this change is needed.\\n\\nSections:\\n- **Why**: 1-2 sentences on the problem or opportunity. What problem does this solve? Why now?\\n- **What Changes**: Bullet list of changes. Be specific about new capabilities, modifications, or removals. Mark breaking changes with **BREAKING**.\\n- **Capabilities**: Identify which specs will be created or modified:\\n  - **New Capabilities**: List capabilities being introduced. Each becomes a new `specs/<capability-path>/spec.md`. Use kebab-case for path segments you introduce (e.g., `user-auth` or `identity/user-auth`) and follow the project's existing spec organization.\\n  - **Modified Capabilities**: List existing capabilities whose REQUIREMENTS are changing. Only include if spec-level behavior changes (not just implementation details). Each needs a delta spec file. Use the exact existing path under `openspec/specs/`. Leave empty if no requirement changes.\\n- **Impact**: Affected code, APIs, dependencies, or systems.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The Capabilities section is critical. It creates the contract between\\nproposal and specs phases. Research existing specs before filling this in:\\nrun `openspec list --specs` for the project's capability inventory, then\\n`openspec show \\\"<spec-id>\\\" --type spec --json --no-scenarios` for any that\\nlook related - that returns a capability's purpose and requirement texts\\nwithout pulling whole spec files into context. Append `--store \\\"<id>\\\"` to\\nboth commands only for a registered standalone store, and keep `--type\\nspec`: a change and a spec sharing a name is otherwise an ambiguous-item\\nerror. `openspec list` without `--specs` lists in-flight changes, not\\nspecs - it never shows what the project already covers. Reuse an existing\\ncapability's exact path instead of introducing a near-duplicate name.\\nThe filtered read is only an overview. Before deciding what is already\\ncovered or what should change, read each relevant spec in full, including\\nscenarios, with `openspec show \\\"<spec-id>\\\" --type spec` (same `--store` rule).\\nEach capability listed here will need a corresponding spec file.\\n\\nEvery change must either declare at least one capability (new or\\nmodified) or explicitly opt out of specs: `openspec validate` rejects a\\nchange with zero deltas unless the change's `.openspec.yaml` sets\\n`skip_specs: true`. Use `skip_specs: true` only when no spec-level\\nbehavior changes (pure refactor, tooling, docs) - specs describe\\nbehavior, so if behavior does not change, no spec should change either.\\nDo not invent a requirement just to satisfy validation.\\n\\nKeep it concise (1-2 pages). Focus on the \\\"why\\\" not the \\\"how\\\" -\\nimplementation details belong in design.md.\\n\\nThis is the foundation - specs, design, and tasks all build on this.\\n\",\n  \"template\": \"# Proposal\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Explain the motivation for this change. What problem does this solve? Why now? -->\\n\\n## What Changes\\n\\n<!-- Describe what will change. Be specific about new capabilities, modifications, or removals. -->\\n\\n## Capabilities\\n\\n### New Capabilities\\n<!-- Capabilities being introduced. Use kebab-case for path segments you introduce\\n     (e.g., user-auth or identity/user-auth) that follow the project's existing\\n     spec organization. Each creates specs/<capability-path>/spec.md. -->\\n- `<capability-path>`: <brief description of what this capability covers>\\n\\n### Modified Capabilities\\n<!-- Existing capabilities whose REQUIREMENTS are changing (not just implementation).\\n     Only list here if spec-level behavior changes. Each needs a delta spec file.\\n     Use the exact existing path under openspec/specs/. Leave empty if no requirement\\n     changes. A change with no capabilities at all (pure refactor, tooling, docs)\\n     must set `skip_specs: true` in its .openspec.yaml - openspec validate rejects\\n     a zero-delta change without that marker. Do not invent a requirement just to\\n     satisfy validation. -->\\n- `<existing-capability-path>`: <what requirement is changing>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<!-- Affected code, APIs, dependencies, systems -->\\n\",\n  \"dependencies\": [],\n  \"unlocks\": [\n    \"specs\",\n    \"design\"\n  ],\n  \"root\": {\n    \"path\": \"/workspace\",\n    \"source\": \"nearest\"\n  }\n}\nsrc/flask/sansio/blueprints.py:405:            self.teardown_request_funcs,\nsrc/flask/sansio/blueprints.py:406:            app.teardown_request_funcs,\nsrc/flask/sansio/blueprints.py:635:        \"\"\"Like :meth:`teardown_request`, but after every request, not only those\nsrc/flask/sansio/blueprints.py:636:        handled by the blueprint. Equivalent to :meth:`.Flask.teardown_request`.\nsrc/flask/sansio/blueprints.py:639:            lambda s: s.app.teardown_request_funcs.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py:360:        self.teardown_appcontext_funcs: list[ft.TeardownCallable] = []\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py:824:    def teardown_appcontext(self, f: T_teardown) -> T_teardown:\nsrc/flask/sansio/app.py:851:        self.teardown_appcontext_funcs.append(f)\nsrc/flask/sansio/scaffold.py:162:        #: To register a function, use the :meth:`teardown_request`\nsrc/flask/sansio/scaffold.py:167:        self.teardown_request_funcs: dict[\nsrc/flask/sansio/scaffold.py:497:        close resources. Use :meth:`teardown_request` for that.\nsrc/flask/sansio/scaffold.py:508:    def teardown_request(self, f: T_teardown) -> T_teardown:\nsrc/flask/sansio/scaffold.py:538:        self.teardown_request_funcs.setdefault(None, []).append(f)\nsrc/flask/app.py:46:from .signals import appcontext_tearing_down\nsrc/flask/app.py:50:from .signals import request_tearing_down\nsrc/flask/app.py:270:            cls.do_teardown_request,\nsrc/flask/app.py:271:            cls.do_teardown_appcontext,\nsrc/flask/app.py:1419:    def do_teardown_request(\nsrc/flask/app.py:1426:        This calls all functions decorated with :meth:`teardown_request`, and\nsrc/flask/app.py:1427:        :meth:`Blueprint.teardown_request` if a blueprint handled the request.\nsrc/flask/app.py:1428:        Finally, the :data:`request_tearing_down` signal is sent.\nsrc/flask/app.py:1437:            if name in self.teardown_request_funcs:\nsrc/flask/app.py:1438:                for func in reversed(self.teardown_request_funcs[name]):\nsrc/flask/app.py:1441:        request_tearing_down.send(self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc)\nsrc/flask/app.py:1443:    def do_teardown_appcontext(\nsrc/flask/app.py:1449:        This calls all functions decorated with :meth:`teardown_appcontext`.\nsrc/flask/app.py:1450:        Then the :data:`appcontext_tearing_down` signal is sent.\nsrc/flask/app.py:1457:        for func in reversed(self.teardown_appcontext_funcs):\nsrc/flask/app.py:1460:        appcontext_tearing_down.send(self, _async_wrapper=self.ensure_sync, exc=exc)\nsrc/flask/signals.py:12:request_tearing_down = _signals.signal(\"request-tearing-down\")\nsrc/flask/s